WEBVTT

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.360
-We are talking about
the model matrix today.

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:05.440
So the model matrix, it takes

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:07.520
you from model space, or we

00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:09.760
like to call it Paul Neale space.

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:12.430
Model space to world space.

00:00:12.430 --> 00:00:15.615
kind of like, Cassidy's
coffee shop right here.

00:00:15.615 --> 00:00:17.735
Cassidy had all these models,

00:00:17.735 --> 00:00:19.855
and she had to decide where

00:00:19.855 --> 00:00:23.535
they are, how they're oriented,
how big and small they are.

00:00:23.535 --> 00:00:26.175
So, yeah, that's what
the model matrix does.

00:00:26.175 --> 00:00:28.815
-So if you look at the
projection matrix as being a

00:00:28.815 --> 00:00:32.090
lens, if you look at the view
matrix as being the orientation

00:00:32.090 --> 00:00:34.010
of a camera, say the camera is

00:00:34.010 --> 00:00:35.930
now pointing at a stage where

00:00:35.930 --> 00:00:38.050
the actors are on stage is

00:00:38.050 --> 00:00:40.170
dictated by their model matrix.

00:00:40.170 --> 00:00:42.330
-Typically, three
things are involved.

00:00:42.330 --> 00:00:45.555
Scale, rotate, translate.

00:00:45.555 --> 00:00:48.435
So I've got them as t r s here.

00:00:48.435 --> 00:00:50.915
So, Cassidy is amazing.

00:00:50.915 --> 00:00:54.115
She has set up motion capture
markers on these three little

00:00:54.115 --> 00:00:57.440
balls to represent these
three transformations.

00:00:57.440 --> 00:01:00.240
So I will start with the scale.

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:03.040
So as I lift this up is that
sandwich getting bigger?

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:04.540
-Yeah. The sandwich is
getting bigger.

00:01:04.540 --> 00:01:06.040
-Here it goes.

00:01:06.040 --> 00:01:07.835
Beautiful.

00:01:07.835 --> 00:01:10.475
Okay. That is the scale.

00:01:10.475 --> 00:01:13.035
Next, the rotation matrix.

00:01:13.035 --> 00:01:16.075
So as I rotate this
is the sandwich?

00:01:16.075 --> 00:01:17.575
Spinning around?

00:01:17.575 --> 00:01:19.075
Oh, look at that.

00:01:19.075 --> 00:01:20.575
There it is.

00:01:20.575 --> 00:01:22.075
I'll spin it back.

00:01:22.075 --> 00:01:23.575
That's the rotation matrix.

00:01:23.575 --> 00:01:25.075
You can kind of imagine
what that's doing.

00:01:25.075 --> 00:01:28.480
And then the last one we have
here is the translate matrix.

00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:32.320
So if I move it forward,
yeah, back where it go.

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:33.820
It's moving around.

00:01:33.820 --> 00:01:36.285
It's translating.

00:01:36.285 --> 00:01:37.785
There you go.

00:01:37.785 --> 00:01:39.285
Wow. So thank you.

00:01:39.285 --> 00:01:40.785
Coded up three matrices.

00:01:40.785 --> 00:01:43.625
And the great thing about
matrices, why I love them, is

00:01:43.625 --> 00:01:46.745
you could just multiply them
together and you get another

00:01:46.745 --> 00:01:48.745
matrix that does
all the operations.

00:01:48.745 --> 00:01:51.850
-I refer to them as the
composite of all of that.

00:01:51.850 --> 00:01:53.350
-Composite. Yeah.

00:01:53.350 --> 00:01:55.450
-So you make one big composite
matrix out of these three

00:01:55.450 --> 00:01:56.950
motions or more.

00:01:56.950 --> 00:01:59.290
-Yeah. Composite is a good
word because when you multiply

00:01:59.290 --> 00:02:02.650
matrices, you have to multiply
them in a certain way.

00:02:02.650 --> 00:02:06.025
You don't just multiply
the numbers willy nilly.

00:02:06.025 --> 00:02:09.145
You got to use a certain I
call it the dump truck method.

00:02:09.145 --> 00:02:11.545
It's a row times a column,
and you add them up.

00:02:11.545 --> 00:02:13.045
So yeah, you got to follow a

00:02:13.045 --> 00:02:14.545
method when you're
multiplying them.

00:02:14.545 --> 00:02:16.045
But yeah.

00:02:16.045 --> 00:02:18.745
-And I like to refer to it as
the composite, because if you

00:02:18.745 --> 00:02:21.950
take many, many matrices and
multiply them all together, you

00:02:21.950 --> 00:02:24.190
get one singular
matrix at the end.

00:02:24.190 --> 00:02:26.910
And that one matrix though is a

00:02:26.910 --> 00:02:28.670
composite of all
of these motions.

00:02:28.670 --> 00:02:30.170
-I think that's why I find

00:02:30.170 --> 00:02:33.025
matrices so
overwhelming sometimes.

00:02:33.025 --> 00:02:36.705
I'm like, what is it doing
if it's made up of all these

00:02:36.705 --> 00:02:38.785
disparate parts?

00:02:38.785 --> 00:02:41.745
That's why I like the
safety of my quaternions.

00:02:41.745 --> 00:02:44.705
They only do one thing and
one thing well, but, we need

00:02:44.705 --> 00:02:48.090
matrices to get objects
onto the screen.

00:02:48.090 --> 00:02:51.210
Now you'll notice I've
put these matrices in a certain

00:02:51.210 --> 00:02:52.730
order next to

00:02:52.730 --> 00:02:55.210
a vertex.

00:02:55.210 --> 00:02:59.130
This is an x y z, and I put
a one at the end for the w.

00:02:59.130 --> 00:03:01.773
This, you can imagine, this is

00:03:01.773 --> 00:03:04.415
one of Paul Neale's vertices, or

00:03:04.415 --> 00:03:06.575
the artist has
given you a model.

00:03:06.575 --> 00:03:08.735
This is one vertex.

00:03:08.735 --> 00:03:11.695
And what you do with this model

00:03:11.695 --> 00:03:14.655
matrix is you multiply it with

00:03:14.655 --> 00:03:16.710
the vertex vector.

00:03:16.710 --> 00:03:18.870
And then this takes us to

00:03:18.870 --> 00:03:21.030
another, kind of not so

00:03:21.030 --> 00:03:23.030
intuitive way of
thinking about this.

00:03:23.030 --> 00:03:25.910
I've written out this
matrix as translate times

00:03:25.910 --> 00:03:27.430
rotate times scale.

00:03:27.430 --> 00:03:30.755
And usually, when you read it
out like that, you think, yeah,

00:03:30.755 --> 00:03:34.195
first you translate, then
you rotate, then you scale.

00:03:34.195 --> 00:03:37.475
We're used to reading
things from left to right.

00:03:37.475 --> 00:03:39.235
-I don't agree.

00:03:39.235 --> 00:03:40.735
-Tell me more.

00:03:40.735 --> 00:03:43.510
-When I'm looking at these
things, I go in this direction.

00:03:43.510 --> 00:03:45.710
So I read them from the right

00:03:45.710 --> 00:03:47.910
hand side towards the left if

00:03:47.910 --> 00:03:49.470
you're thinking about it from

00:03:49.470 --> 00:03:51.030
the point of view of the model.

00:03:51.030 --> 00:03:53.190
If you're thinking of it
from the point of view of the

00:03:53.190 --> 00:03:55.750
camera, then you
read it your way.

00:03:55.750 --> 00:03:57.250
-You know what?

00:03:57.250 --> 00:03:58.750
I love it when Scott and I

00:03:58.750 --> 00:04:00.250
disagree because
we end up agreeing.

00:04:00.250 --> 00:04:01.750
I totally agree with
everything you just said.

00:04:01.765 --> 00:04:04.885
Because, what I was
trying to say is, yes, you

00:04:04.885 --> 00:04:06.385
scale first.

00:04:06.385 --> 00:04:07.885
Notice these two are
next to each other.

00:04:07.885 --> 00:04:09.840
So the scale happens first,

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:13.440
then you the rotate happens in
imagine you multiply these two

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:15.840
things together, the
scale and the vertex.

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:18.960
You end up with another vector.

00:04:18.960 --> 00:04:22.080
You multiply the rotation
by this new vector, you get

00:04:22.080 --> 00:04:25.635
another vector, and then
lastly, the translate.

00:04:25.635 --> 00:04:29.395
So, again, we disagree and
yet agree at the same time.

00:04:29.395 --> 00:04:31.395
Okay. Now you need to explain

00:04:31.395 --> 00:04:33.395
a little bit more about this

00:04:33.395 --> 00:04:35.658
camera move because, you're

00:04:35.658 --> 00:04:37.920
saying for a camera, you do

00:04:37.920 --> 00:04:40.720
read it translate,
rotate, scale.

00:04:40.720 --> 00:04:42.220
-Well, the reason we're

00:04:42.220 --> 00:04:45.440
agreeing, even though
my way is easier,

00:04:45.440 --> 00:04:48.240
if I'm looking at
just the model

00:04:48.240 --> 00:04:51.040
then I want to say how big

00:04:51.040 --> 00:04:52.540
is that model?

00:04:52.540 --> 00:04:55.855
Then where am I going to
position the model in terms of

00:04:55.855 --> 00:04:57.355
its rotation?

00:04:57.355 --> 00:05:00.815
And then where do I place the
model with its translation?

00:05:00.815 --> 00:05:03.055
Now, if I'm looking at it
from the point of view of the

00:05:03.055 --> 00:05:05.455
camera, remember the camera's
at the origin, looking down the

00:05:05.455 --> 00:05:07.410
negative Z axis.

00:05:07.410 --> 00:05:10.450
And so if I'm looking at it
that way, where is everything?

00:05:10.450 --> 00:05:13.570
Well, first I have to translate
the whole universe in front of

00:05:13.570 --> 00:05:15.730
the camera because the
camera doesn't move.

00:05:15.730 --> 00:05:19.090
Then I have to rotate, then I
can scale, and then I can apply

00:05:19.090 --> 00:05:20.590
that to this.

00:05:20.590 --> 00:05:22.765
So if you're thinking from
the camera point of view, from

00:05:22.765 --> 00:05:25.965
viewing it as the camera,
you read that way.

00:05:25.965 --> 00:05:28.045
If you do it from the model's

00:05:28.045 --> 00:05:30.125
perspective, you read this way.

00:05:30.125 --> 00:05:34.125
Now another way that I do it
in class is we'll read this way

00:05:34.125 --> 00:05:35.640
when we're dealing
with the models.

00:05:35.640 --> 00:05:37.680
Then when we're talking camera

00:05:37.680 --> 00:05:39.720
moves, you read that way as we

00:05:39.720 --> 00:05:41.220
read in the camera.

00:05:41.220 --> 00:05:43.960
Then right there at the end of
the model matrix to the view

00:05:43.960 --> 00:05:46.600
matrix is where the
separation occurs.

00:05:46.600 --> 00:05:50.265
As the view matrix and the
projection matrix make up with

00:05:50.265 --> 00:05:51.945
what you call a camera.

00:05:51.945 --> 00:05:54.425
Even though the camera doesn't
move, it's at the origin

00:05:54.425 --> 00:05:56.265
looking down the
negative z axis.

00:05:56.265 --> 00:05:59.064
Of course 
I agree with you on that.

00:05:59.064 --> 00:06:01.511
What Scott was talking about is

00:06:01.511 --> 00:06:04.900
the next matrix along,
the view matrix.

00:06:04.900 --> 00:06:06.765
If I move the scale out of
the way for a second, with a

00:06:06.765 --> 00:06:08.265
camera, you don't scale.

00:06:08.265 --> 00:06:09.765
You just rotate, translate.

00:06:09.765 --> 00:06:10.667
And like like Scott's saying,

00:06:10.667 --> 00:06:11.570
the first thing you 
want to do is

00:06:11.570 --> 00:06:13.650
translate back to
the origin.

00:06:13.650 --> 00:06:15.730
And then rotate back so that

00:06:15.730 --> 00:06:17.395
you're looking
down negative z.

00:06:17.395 --> 00:06:19.995
The crazy thing is if you

00:06:19.995 --> 00:06:22.595
invert this matrix, 
t times r

00:06:22.595 --> 00:06:24.635
inverted, it actually swaps

00:06:24.635 --> 00:06:26.675
them round with an

00:06:26.675 --> 00:06:28.175
inverse as well.

00:06:28.175 --> 00:06:30.430
So we agree yet again.

00:06:30.430 --> 00:06:33.230
Unbelievable. So yes,
order does matter.

00:06:33.230 --> 00:06:36.190
If these were just regular
numbers, one times two times

00:06:36.190 --> 00:06:37.690
three, you could you could

00:06:37.690 --> 00:06:39.190
change the order
of multiplication.

00:06:39.190 --> 00:06:40.690
It doesn't change the result,

00:06:40.690 --> 00:06:44.190
but because these are
matrices transformations,

00:06:44.190 --> 00:06:46.885
it really does matter
which one is first.

00:06:46.885 --> 00:06:48.965
Typically, 
when I'm making a

00:06:48.965 --> 00:06:51.045
model matrix, 
I have like a

00:06:51.045 --> 00:06:54.645
physics object that has a
position, an orientation.

00:06:54.645 --> 00:06:57.205
I could have a scale as well.

00:06:57.205 --> 00:06:58.805
I usually return a model

00:06:58.805 --> 00:07:01.660
matrix, which does this
exact multiplication.

00:07:01.660 --> 00:07:04.460
The translate times a rotate
times a scale, and that's my

00:07:04.460 --> 00:07:05.960
model matrix.

00:07:05.960 --> 00:07:08.380
-But if you wanted to do
something more complicated, for

00:07:08.380 --> 00:07:10.660
example, if I take an object at

00:07:10.660 --> 00:07:12.940
the origin, rotate it a little

00:07:12.940 --> 00:07:16.545
bit, and then translate it, so
I would change its orientation

00:07:16.545 --> 00:07:18.585
from here to say here,

00:07:18.585 --> 00:07:20.625
just in a rotate.

00:07:20.625 --> 00:07:22.785
Then to translate it, I would

00:07:22.785 --> 00:07:25.025
take that, rotate
it, translate it.

00:07:25.025 --> 00:07:27.425
Now what would happen
if I flip those around?

00:07:27.425 --> 00:07:29.750
So what would happen is
I would translate it.

00:07:29.750 --> 00:07:31.430
Now the rotate, but the rotate

00:07:31.430 --> 00:07:33.430
is still occurring
at the origin.

00:07:33.430 --> 00:07:35.750
So now it's going like that.

00:07:35.750 --> 00:07:37.250
It's going to rotate around.

00:07:37.250 --> 00:07:39.270
Like the moon
going around the earth.

00:07:39.270 --> 00:07:41.645
But maybe that's what I want.

00:07:41.645 --> 00:07:45.485
So it doesn't always have
to be translate, rotate, scale.

00:07:45.485 --> 00:07:48.845
It could be translate,
rotate, rotate, translate.

00:07:48.845 --> 00:07:51.165
A whole bunch of them.

00:07:51.165 --> 00:07:52.665
-Yeah. True.

00:07:52.665 --> 00:07:54.165
Lots of moves.

00:07:54.165 --> 00:07:57.165
And I wonder if we can maybe we

00:07:57.165 --> 00:07:58.665
can try and
demonstrate this idea.

00:07:58.665 --> 00:08:00.880
So if I understand what
you're seeing is if we rotate

00:08:00.880 --> 00:08:04.640
the sandwich look at that.

00:08:04.640 --> 00:08:08.485
It's on its side, and
then translate it.

00:08:08.485 --> 00:08:09.985
Off it goes.

00:08:09.985 --> 00:08:11.485
So that that
looks pretty good.

00:08:11.485 --> 00:08:14.085
I'll rotate it back.

00:08:14.085 --> 00:08:16.805
So that would be if I get
rid of the scale for a second,

00:08:16.805 --> 00:08:19.403
that's a rotate,

00:08:19.403 --> 00:08:22.000
then a translate.

00:08:22.000 --> 00:08:23.600
But let's see how this works.

00:08:23.600 --> 00:08:25.280
If I was to swap them around,

00:08:26.980 --> 00:08:28.640
translate first.

00:08:28.640 --> 00:08:30.480
Okay. Let's
see how this works.

00:08:30.480 --> 00:08:32.600
So I'm going to 
translate the

00:08:32.600 --> 00:08:34.720
sound translate the sound wave.

00:08:34.720 --> 00:08:36.415
There it goes.

00:08:36.415 --> 00:08:38.495
And now if I rotate

00:08:38.495 --> 00:08:40.575
Cassidy, you're a genius.

00:08:40.575 --> 00:08:42.075
Look at it.

00:08:42.075 --> 00:08:44.175
It's not rotating about
its centre anymore.

00:08:44.175 --> 00:08:46.815
It's rotating about the
