00:11:12.600 --> 00:11:14.680
you do want to scale the

00:11:14.680 --> 00:11:18.120
vertices, but I'm thinking
about the normals.

00:11:18.120 --> 00:11:20.520
We also have to do
stuff with normals.

00:11:20.520 --> 00:11:22.587
For the normals, it has a w

00:11:22.587 --> 00:11:24.655
of zero, and actually, that's

00:11:24.655 --> 00:11:27.935
enough to get rid of
translate, actually.

00:11:27.935 --> 00:11:31.135
But when there's a
let's think about this.

00:11:31.135 --> 00:11:32.895
Maybe if I switch this,
oh, I

00:11:32.895 --> 00:11:34.395
don't have a zero
here, actually.

00:11:34.395 --> 00:11:37.424
But you'd have to just
imagine there's a zero here.

00:11:39.638 --> 00:11:42.430
If there is no...

00:11:42.430 --> 00:11:46.350
Well, if it's a uniform scale,
you can actually just ignore it

00:11:46.350 --> 00:11:49.470
and just multiply your
model matrix well.

00:11:49.470 --> 00:11:51.070
-Renormalize it.

00:11:51.070 --> 00:11:52.894
-Oh, yes. Of course.

00:11:52.894 --> 00:11:56.152
You've got to
renormalize the normal.

00:11:56.152 --> 00:11:58.570
Yeah. Your normals have
to have unit length.

00:11:58.570 --> 00:12:00.405
Otherwise, the lighting
is not going to look good.

00:12:00.405 --> 00:12:03.045
So,
 if you're not doing a

00:12:03.045 --> 00:12:05.685
squish or a stretch, 
just grab

00:12:05.685 --> 00:12:08.448
your model matrix 
and model it

00:12:08.448 --> 00:12:11.210
with the normal, 
but the w is

00:12:11.210 --> 00:12:12.710
going to be zero.

00:12:12.710 --> 00:12:15.530
That just switches
off translations.

00:12:15.530 --> 00:12:19.290
Things get a bit trickier when
you like to squish and stretch

00:12:19.290 --> 00:12:20.970
like Paul does and Cassidy.

00:12:20.970 --> 00:12:23.945
Yep. You you do like squishing
and stretching stuff.

00:12:23.945 --> 00:12:25.545
So imagine your scale

00:12:25.545 --> 00:12:28.825
is non-uniform.

00:12:28.825 --> 00:12:32.505
Imagine I had a sphere, and I
just squished it down into a

00:12:32.505 --> 00:12:34.530
flat piece of paper.

00:12:34.530 --> 00:12:38.450
Yeah. It's a little bit more
complicated to work out what

00:12:38.450 --> 00:12:39.950
the normal is.

00:12:39.950 --> 00:12:41.450
There's a bunch of moves.

00:12:41.450 --> 00:12:43.510
The first move is called

00:12:43.510 --> 00:12:45.570
the inverse transpose.

00:12:45.570 --> 00:12:48.770
So you grab this matrix,
you know, t times r times s,

00:12:48.770 --> 00:12:50.350
inverse transpose.

00:12:50.350 --> 00:12:53.925
The great thing about inverse
transpose, it does nothing to

00:12:53.925 --> 00:12:56.325
the rotation because

00:12:56.325 --> 00:13:00.325
the inverse of a rotation
matrix is the transpose.

00:13:00.325 --> 00:13:01.825
Is the transpose.

00:13:01.825 --> 00:13:03.325
Isn't that wild?

00:13:03.325 --> 00:13:04.825
-That's 'cause it's orthogonal.
-Yes!

00:13:04.825 --> 00:13:06.325
-Cassidy told me about
that just a few minutes ago.

00:13:06.325 --> 00:13:07.825
-Nice. Okay.

00:13:07.825 --> 00:13:09.325
So there's this move.

00:13:09.325 --> 00:13:11.000
Inverse transpose.

00:13:11.000 --> 00:13:13.560
Oh, and I should probably
explain what a transpose is.

00:13:13.560 --> 00:13:15.895
When you've got a matrix,

00:13:15.895 --> 00:13:18.775
it's made up of
rows and columns.

00:13:18.775 --> 00:13:21.095
We use four by four matrices,

00:13:21.095 --> 00:13:23.335
so four rows, four columns.

00:13:23.335 --> 00:13:25.375
The transpose just means

00:13:25.375 --> 00:13:27.415
every row is now the column.

00:13:27.415 --> 00:13:30.375
So the first row is now the
first column, second row is

00:13:30.375 --> 00:13:32.910
second column, blah blah
blah, just like that.

00:13:32.910 --> 00:13:35.030
So doing the inverse transpose

00:13:35.030 --> 00:13:37.150
does nothing to the rotation,

00:13:37.150 --> 00:13:39.150
and that's exactly what
you want for a normal.

00:13:39.150 --> 00:13:40.650
You do want the normal to

00:13:40.650 --> 00:13:42.510
rotate as you rotate the object.

00:13:42.510 --> 00:13:44.355
There's another move we do.

00:13:44.355 --> 00:13:48.035
We do, you inverse it,
transpose it, and then you

00:13:48.035 --> 00:13:51.075
knock it down to a
three by three matrix.

00:13:51.075 --> 00:13:54.275
Because if you inverse the
translation, it's like just

00:13:54.275 --> 00:13:55.775
going the other way.

00:13:55.775 --> 00:13:58.675
If you translated three to the
right, inverse it is three to

00:13:58.675 --> 00:14:01.720
the left, which is not
helpful for the normal.

00:14:01.720 --> 00:14:04.840
And then when you transpose
it, it makes it even worse.

00:14:04.840 --> 00:14:07.360
Your translate is typically in

00:14:07.360 --> 00:14:09.880
the final column of the matrix,

00:14:09.880 --> 00:14:13.400
but if it's a transpose,
now it's the final row.

00:14:13.400 --> 00:14:15.295
Oh my goodness.

00:14:15.295 --> 00:14:16.795
That looks pretty ugly.

00:14:16.795 --> 00:14:18.295
So you know what?

00:14:18.295 --> 00:14:19.795
We're like, forget it.

00:14:19.795 --> 00:14:21.295
And we we just chop it off.

00:14:21.295 --> 00:14:23.119
We just chop off the last
row and the last column.

00:14:23.119 --> 00:14:25.555
So, basically, we're
not in four d space anymore.

00:14:25.555 --> 00:14:27.715
We're in three
d space at that point.

00:14:27.715 --> 00:14:29.733
So inverse transpose

00:14:29.733 --> 00:14:31.750
rotation is fine.

00:14:31.750 --> 00:14:35.030
We chop it down to a three
by three matrix, translations

00:14:35.030 --> 00:14:37.430
disappear, and then the last

00:14:37.430 --> 00:14:39.688
pesky thing is the

00:14:39.688 --> 00:14:41.945
non-uniform scale.

00:14:41.945 --> 00:14:45.945
So, if you imagine in your
mind's eye, if you have

00:14:45.945 --> 00:14:47.985
this sphere which has normal

00:14:47.985 --> 00:14:50.025
directions perpendicular to the

00:14:50.025 --> 00:14:52.305
surface, 
and as you squish the

00:14:52.305 --> 00:14:54.585
sphere down, 
you want these

00:14:54.585 --> 00:14:57.572
normals to start pointing up

00:14:57.572 --> 00:15:00.560
as the sphere kind of squishes

00:15:00.560 --> 00:15:02.160
down in that way.

00:15:02.160 --> 00:15:03.660
-I see that.

00:15:03.660 --> 00:15:05.160
So they want to 
come in like this.

00:15:05.160 --> 00:15:06.660
-Yes. Yeah.

00:15:06.660 --> 00:15:08.160
You you want it to
come the other way.

00:15:08.160 --> 00:15:10.202
So one way of imagining the

00:15:10.202 --> 00:15:12.245
inverse transpose, it's like

00:15:12.245 --> 00:15:14.725
you're inversing the scale and

00:15:14.725 --> 00:15:17.205
then changing what it's scaling.

00:15:17.205 --> 00:15:19.045
Is it the x, y, or the z?

00:15:19.045 --> 00:15:22.485
And what that effectively does
is that as the shape stretches

00:15:22.485 --> 00:15:26.450
outwards, the normals
point inwards.

00:15:26.450 --> 00:15:28.670
It's actually pretty magical.

00:15:28.690 --> 00:15:30.610
And we were doing the
math the other day.

00:15:30.610 --> 00:15:32.270
It worked out.

00:15:32.270 --> 00:15:33.770
-It does work.

00:15:33.770 --> 00:15:35.270
-And I'm still stunned 
it works.

00:15:35.270 --> 00:15:38.050
So if you're dealing with
normals, and normals have a w

00:15:38.050 --> 00:15:40.333
of zero,

00:15:40.333 --> 00:15:42.535
there's kind of,

00:15:42.535 --> 00:15:44.615
it's a bit 
of a strange move.

00:15:44.615 --> 00:15:47.015
Here's our model matrix.

00:15:47.015 --> 00:15:50.695
Scale first, rotate, and then
translate when you multiply it,

00:15:50.695 --> 00:15:52.515
t r s.

00:15:52.515 --> 00:15:54.015
So you got to
 do some moves.

00:15:54.015 --> 00:15:56.252
You got to inverse this whole
matrix,

00:15:56.252 --> 00:15:58.490
transpose it, and then

00:15:58.490 --> 00:16:01.530
get rid of that final
row and final column.

00:16:01.530 --> 00:16:04.570
And then your lighting is
going to look really good.

00:16:04.570 --> 00:16:06.070
-Yeah. Every time.

00:16:06.070 --> 00:16:09.770
-Yeah. Or what you could do is
not do a drawn uniform scale,

00:16:09.770 --> 00:16:13.705
and then you just,
like, multiply as usual.

00:16:13.705 --> 00:16:17.385
T r and then your normal w zero.

00:16:17.385 --> 00:16:20.105
That's why I like
uniform scales.

00:16:20.105 --> 00:16:22.585
Let's go over this
one more time.

00:16:22.585 --> 00:16:25.067
So we have vertices and normals

00:16:25.067 --> 00:16:27.550
given to us by the artist.

00:16:27.550 --> 00:16:31.390
So we're starting in model
space and then using something

00:16:31.390 --> 00:16:33.590
called a model matrix to go to

00:16:33.590 --> 00:16:36.910
the next space, world space.

00:16:36.910 --> 00:16:39.495
It's typically made
up of three things.

00:16:39.495 --> 00:16:42.375
There is the scale, which is

00:16:42.375 --> 00:16:44.775
making the sandwich
bigger and smaller.

00:16:44.775 --> 00:16:47.415
There you go.

00:16:47.415 --> 00:16:48.915
There's the rotate.

00:16:48.915 --> 00:16:51.175
Look at that sandwich
go spinning around.

00:16:51.175 --> 00:16:52.835
Looks delicious.

00:16:54.790 --> 00:16:57.710
And a translate as you move

00:16:57.710 --> 00:17:00.630
the sandwich 
closer to your mouth

00:17:00.630 --> 00:17:02.150
so you can eat it.

00:17:02.150 --> 00:17:03.830
So those are the three matrices.

00:17:03.830 --> 00:17:05.795
Order matters, though.

00:17:05.795 --> 00:17:07.295
-So read it this way.

00:17:07.295 --> 00:17:08.795
-Oh, yeah. Go ahead.

00:17:08.795 --> 00:17:10.295
-No, go ahead.

00:17:10.295 --> 00:17:12.089
-Read it that way. Yes.

00:17:12.089 --> 00:17:14.435
-I finally convinced you.

00:17:14.435 --> 00:17:16.088
-Yes. I agree with you.

00:17:16.088 --> 00:17:18.179
You have to read it that way.

00:17:18.179 --> 00:17:20.795
You want to scale, then
rotate, then translate.

00:17:20.795 --> 00:17:22.295
I find it useful to
have a little prop.

00:17:22.295 --> 00:17:25.230
If you have a vertex there,
you can see right away which

00:17:25.230 --> 00:17:27.070
multiplication happens first.

00:17:27.070 --> 00:17:29.070
Also, order matters.

00:17:29.070 --> 00:17:31.950
So oh, we can try
the demonstration again.

00:17:31.950 --> 00:17:35.215
Yeah. If I trans,
 oh, translate

00:17:35.215 --> 00:17:38.095
the sandwich first, off it goes.

00:17:38.095 --> 00:17:39.995
I think it's behind me now.

00:17:39.995 --> 00:17:42.415
Trying to get it over there.

00:17:42.415 --> 00:17:43.915
There we go.

00:17:43.915 --> 00:17:45.415
I translated the sandwich.

00:17:45.455 --> 00:17:46.955
Thank you.

00:17:46.955 --> 00:17:49.310
Now when I'm rotating
it, it looks weird.

00:17:49.310 --> 00:17:51.470
Well, it is rotating, 
but it's

00:17:51.470 --> 00:17:53.630
rotating about the origin, 
and

00:17:53.630 --> 00:17:55.710
we've just moved the origin.

00:17:55.710 --> 00:17:57.830
So it is important to

00:17:57.830 --> 00:17:59.950
understand if you swap the

00:17:59.950 --> 00:18:02.187
order of these, if you do RTS,

00:18:02.187 --> 00:18:04.425
we haven't even talked about

00:18:04.425 --> 00:18:06.265
how scale can impact it as well.

00:18:06.265 --> 00:18:08.985
So, yeah, just be careful, but
that might be something you

00:18:08.985 --> 00:18:10.485
want to do.
-Yeah.

00:18:10.485 --> 00:18:14.040
You want might want
the moon to orbit the Earth.

00:18:14.040 --> 00:18:15.540
Oh, go ahead.

00:18:15.540 --> 00:18:17.040
-No. I'm just looking.

00:18:17.040 --> 00:18:19.158
Be careful when you're
doing the scalings.

00:18:19.158 --> 00:18:21.640
If you do a scale in the wrong
order, then a translate, you

00:18:21.640 --> 00:18:23.240
would scale the translation.

00:18:23.240 --> 00:18:24.740
-Right. Yep.

00:18:24.740 --> 00:18:27.575
I guess what I've got right
here, we translate first, then

00:18:27.575 --> 00:18:30.295
scale, so you're moving
even further away.

00:18:30.295 --> 00:18:32.035
-Or closer.

00:18:32.035 --> 00:18:33.535
You could be scaling it smaller.

00:18:33.535 --> 00:18:35.035
-Usually, have a student coming

00:18:35.035 --> 00:18:37.000
up to us and say,
why isn't it working?

00:18:37.000 --> 00:18:40.360
And, yeah, it's kind of
subtle how you multiply these

00:18:40.360 --> 00:18:42.200
three things together.

00:18:42.200 --> 00:18:45.320
But, okay,
that's the model matrix.

00:18:45.320 --> 00:18:48.200
We talked about
parenting as well.

00:18:48.200 --> 00:18:50.600
Your parent can have a model
matrix, and then the child

00:18:50.600 --> 00:18:52.100
follows the parent.

00:18:52.100 --> 00:18:55.335
And if you are dealing with
normals, you always want the

00:18:55.335 --> 00:18:57.735
normal to be perpendicular
to the surface.

00:18:57.735 --> 00:18:59.555
So there is a move.

00:18:59.575 --> 00:19:02.935
Maybe I'll just say, just
be careful when you're when

00:19:02.935 --> 00:19:06.874
you're taking a normal from
model space to world space.

00:19:06.874 --> 00:19:08.374
-Perfect.

00:19:08.374 --> 00:19:09.874
-Yeah. Thanks so much.

00:19:09.874 --> 00:19:12.874
We disagreed, and
then we agreed.

00:19:12.874 --> 00:19:14.374
-As always.